w_CRH(problem) = |I(ρ̂)| + 2·z_{1−α/2}·ŝe, with ρ̂ chosen ONCE on the calibration split by the nested-family rule above and then frozen.

Every competing row gets exactly one scalar tuned on the same split by the same rule: methods with a knob use it; methods without one (DoubleML on the elicited set, TabPFN, CausalPFN, Do-PFN, enumeration of total effects, blanket hedge) get a multiplicative half-width scale s, and s is the smallest value on a grid achieving the target coverage on the calibration split. No method is allowed a per-row post hoc knob.

HEADLINE = mean width at 95% coverage ON THE DAMAGED STRATUM (θ̂0 ≠ τ*). Marginal width and coverage are printed beside it, never instead of it. Reporting only the marginal number is the gaming surface the audit identified and it is closed by the table's own layout.

MANDATORY ROWS, because two of them can erase the contribution:
  · ball of radius b, i.e. c ≡ 0 (direction (1) alone) — the row that decides whether the selection is real;
  · hop-distance cost (the locality law as a one-line ranker) — if a learned ranker does not beat it, the contribution is the locality law;
  · elicited set + constant inflation calibrated to 95%;
  · blanket hedge over the whole equivalence class.

MEASURED ON MY 452-PROBLEM PROBE (true CPDAGs, population estimands, no sampling noise), at ρ̂=3 calibrated on the damaged stratum: CRH 0.2973 · ball(b≤2) 0.3008 · blanket 0.3252 · elicited+constant inflation 1.1973 at marginal calibration (which reaches only 0.531 coverage on the damaged stratum) and 4.1638 when honestly calibrated on the damaged stratum. So the CRH beats the blanket by 8.6% and the constant inflation by 4×, but beats direction (1)'s ball by 1.2%.

STRATIFICATION THE TABLE MUST CARRY: 56.9% of problems have zero blanket width — the equivalence class already identifies the effect and every method scores perfectly. The mean-width column is dominated by those, so the table reports it split by whether the blanket width is zero.

